 "summary": "Francis Tiffany describes a journey round the world made with a friend, Edwin B. Haskell, to whom the book is dedicated. They cross the United States by rail and the Pacific by steamer to Japan, where about a third of the book is spent: Yokohama, Kamakura and its great Buddha, the Hakone district, Nikko, Japanese art and manners, and the question of missions. They go on to Shanghai, Hong Kong and Canton, Singapore, and Ceylon, then through India from Calcutta and Darjeeling to Benares, Lucknow, Agra, Delhi, Jeypore, and Mount Abu. The last parts cover Egypt and the Nile, Palestine, Baalbec and Damascus, Smyrna, Constantinople, and Athens. The book is a series of short numbered sections, each with a title in the printed contents, in which description is mixed with reflection on religion, history, and art.",
